1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 Nb6 ( 8... Ba6 { is the main line, but I recommend to play the text move, because it is at least as good and requires less memorization. } ) 
9. Nc3 { This is the most straightforward continuation and the starting point of this chapter (all the alternatives were covered in the previous chapters). -KEY- } Qe6 10. b3 $6 a5 { This standard method works just as well here as in the 8.Nd2 variation. } 11. Bb2 a4 12. O-O-O axb3 13. axb3 Ba3 $15 { Black has easy play against White's weak queenside. } 
