1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 Nb6 { (8...Ba6 is the main line, but I recommend to play the text move, because it is at least as good and requires less memorization.) } 9. Nc3 { This is the most straightforward continuation and the starting point of this chapter (all the alternatives were covered in the previous chapters). } Qe6 10. Bd2 { Playing for a quick long castle. -KEY- } Ba6 11. b3 Ba3 $1 { Stopping the above-mentioned plan. } 12. Qe4 $6 { This move has been played most often by White, but Black has a powerful reply. } O-O 13. Bd3 f5 $1 { The key move. } 14. exf6 Qxe4+ 15. Bxe4 d5 $1 { Energetically opening the lines, taking advantage of the White king's vulnerable position in the centre. } 16. Bf3 ( { White erred with } 16. cxd5 $2 { and was already lost after } Bb2 { in Papp-Batsiashvili, Gibraltar 2018. } ) 
Rae8+ 17. Kd1 Rxf6 $15 { White is under pressure. } 
